 * zero-contention instruction memory, otherwise declared in every
 * libDaisy linker script's MEMORY block but never actually used by any
 * of them. This exists because measuring real hardware after moving to
 * BOOT_QSPI showed the audio callback's worst-case timing rising from
 * <50% to 69% of its real-time budget for the same DSP workload --
 * QSPI's execute-in-place latency is real, not just a theoretical
 * concern the instruction cache fully absorbs. The real-time call graph
 * (AudioCallback(), LooperLayer::Process(), TempoClock::Process()/
 * RenderClick()) is tagged to live here so it stays exactly as fast as
 * it was on internal flash, while everything else (UI, SD, file I/O)
 * stays on QSPI where the extra space actually matters. See DESIGN.md
 * for the full story.
 */

